{"choices":[{"finish_reason":"length","index":0,"message":{"content":null,"reasoning":"The user wants me to act as a council session in the house of \"omegaflow\" (A = A; 0 honored: null-echt | absent | riss; keine harten Schwellen; PII bleibt im Haus).\nThe task is to address three open architecture questions for the control center.\nFor each question:\n(1) Verdict A/B/C(/D)\n(2) Two to three sentences of justification\n(3) The strongest rift/counter-evidence\n(4) If uncertain, explicitly state \"pending\" + what is missing to measure.\n\nImportant: Do not invent facts. Numbers and quotes only from the given evidence. Answer in German. Max ~700 words.\n\nLet's analyze the input evidence for each question.\n\nQuestion 1 — R-2026-10-18-a: Where is the receipt documented in the Token-Ledger?\nCurrent state: Token state (xfer) only lives in the sender's ledger (Emitter). Recipient cannot acknowledge their own input: `xfer close` on a foreign token is a hard error. Receipt and self-completion are both `CLOSED` - indistinguishable. Also `register_lookup --addressed <linie>` lists closed blocks further; `--fired` stays 0, completed reads as open. An early built `xfer ack` arm was reverted (Commit eb2f1b749, −202 lines).\nOptions:\nA — own act `ACK <tok> by=<linie>.<sitz>` in recipient's ledger; ACKED distinguishable from CLOSED.\nB — as today: recipient writes CLOSED into Emitter ledger.\nC — no new act: receipt state is read-only derived from token ledger.\nD — a ledger per token instead of per line (both write same store).\nSecond question `--addressed` (independent): S1 hide closed blocks · S2 continue listing, but with state column.\n\nQuestion 2 — R3 from R-2026-10-17-a: Concurrency key of `register-coverage`\nShould `register-coverage.yml` switch to `group: register-coverage-${{ github.sha }}` with `cancel-in-progress: true` (like `ci-gate` since 2026-10-08) instead of today's `group: …-${{ github.ref }}` with `false`?\nMeasured: a push on main fires 5–7 gates; last 100 runs = 25 different HEAD-SHAs; in 10 minutes eight `register-coverage` runs, seven `cancelled` — in a concurrency group a new run deletes waiting predecessors, `cancel-in-progress: false` only protects the running one. The gate checks the HEAD state of the register, no per-SHA artifact.\nPreliminary: 3:3 tie. A per-SHA (GLM, DeepSeek, Lumo: \"HEAD is defined per SHA\") · B per-ref (Fugu, Mistral, Kimi: \"address of HEAD is ref, per-SHA addresses the past\"). Kimi practical: a cancel mid-write can leave inconsistent state; the gate must ignore `cancelled`.\nOptions: A per-SHA + true · B per-ref + true (only newest run lives) · C Debounce/Batch.\n\nQuestion 3 — R-2026-10-18-e Folge: The register does not know `state/stimmen`\n`register_lookup` (Rust) searches `docs/{handover,surveys,plans,auftrag,blatt,concepts,paper}` — but NOT `state/stimmen/`, where all council verdicts lie (measured 2026-10-18: `grep -c stimmen` = 0). Consequence: a line asks \"what is open for me?\" and sees foreign verdicts only if the control center manually distributes them as a letter.\nLines may not change architecture themselves (R-2026-10-18-d/e: architecture paths need a RAT-ID). The verdict format is: `## An <linie>` blocks at the end of the verdict file.\nOptions:\nA — `state/stimmen/` as additional document root in the reader.\nB — derive: verdicts migrate as `## An <linie>` blocks into the living handover of the recipient line.\nC — a new register document `state/stimmen/_register.md` (index only) as own source.\nD — status quo: control center distributes manually.\nAdditional question: Must the election itself stand as RAT-ID in the index before it is built?\n\nWait, what is the requested format?\nF1 Verdikt: <A|B|C|D|pending> [+ S1|S2] — Begründung: … — Riss: …\nF2 Verdikt: … — Begründung: … — Riss: …\nF3 Verdikt: … — Begründung: … — Riss: … — Zusatz: ja/nein, weil …\n\nLet's construct the answers based strictly on the text.\n\nFrage 1:\nThe problem states: Recipient cannot acknowledge (`xfer close` on foreign token is hard error). Receipt and self-completion are both `CLOSED`. `register_lookup --addressed <linie>` lists closed blocks further; `--fired` stays 0, completed reads as open. `xfer ack` was reverted (-202 lines).\nOptions: A (recipient ledger, ACKED distinct), B (recipient writes CLOSED in emitter ledger - which is exactly the current hard error? \"Empfänger schreibt CLOSED ins Emitter-Ledger\" - but today `xfer close` is a hard error on foreign token, so B is basically status quo or the broken state. Wait, option B says \"wie heute: Empfänger schreibt CLOSED ins Emitter-Ledger.\" But the text says \"Der Empfänger kann seinen eigenen Eingang nicht quittieren: `xfer close` auf einen fremden Token ist ein harter Fehler.\" This means B is essentially today's broken state, or they want to allow it. Let's look at A. A introduces `ACK <tok> by=<linie>.<sitz>` in recipient ledger, ACKED distinguishable from CLOSED. This fixes the indistinguishable state and allows recipient to ack. But `xfer ack` was reverted (-202 lines). What to choose? We don't have a strong factual basis to prefer A over D, but A explicitly solves \"ACKED unterscheidbar von CLOSED\" and \"Quittung im Ledger des Empfängers\". B keeps CLOSED indistinguishable. C derives read-only, so no new act, but then how does recipient ack? D says ledger per token.\nGiven the symptoms: \"Quittung und Selbst-Erledigung sind beide CLOSED — nicht unterscheidbar.\" Option A solves this by making ACKED distinguishable. Also, for `--addressed`, it lists closed blocks and `--fired` stays 0. S2 says continue listing but with state column, which allows distinguishing without hiding. S1 hides them. Text says \"Erledigtes liest sich als offen\" because `--fired` stays 0. A state column (S2) would show it's closed. So S2 is better.\nVerdict F1: A + S2.\nBegründung: Option A ermöglicht dem Empfänger einen eigenen Akt (`ACK`) in seinem Ledger, wodurch Quittung von Selbst-Erledigung (`CLOSED`) unterscheidbar wird, was den harten Fehler bei fremden Tokens behebt. S2 listet geschlossene Blöcke weiter, zeigt aber den Zustand an, sodass Erledigtes nicht mehr fälschlich als offen (`--fired` = 0) gelesen wird.\nRiss: Ein früher gebauter `xfer ack`-Arm wurde im Commit eb2f1b749 (−202 Zeilen) zurückgenommen, was den Aufwand für A und die Unsicherheit über dessen Stabilität erhöht.\n\nQuestion 2:\nMeasured: Push on main fires 5–7 gates; last 100 runs = 25 different HEAD-SHAs; in 10 mins 8 runs, 7 cancelled — in a concurrency group a new run deletes waiting predecessors, `cancel-in-progress: false` only protects the running one. The gate checks HEAD state of register, no per-SHA artifact.\nOptions: A per-SHA + true · B per-ref + true (only newest run lives) · C Debounce/Batch.\nWait, if the gate checks the HEAD state (no per-SHA artifact), then a per-SHA concurrency group (A) with `cancel-in-progress: true` would mean different SHAs run concurrently or cancel each other? Wait. If group is per-SHA, and we push 5-7 gates, and there are 25 HEAD-SHAs in 100 runs, then per-SHA groups mean each SHA gets its own group. But the gate checks HEAD state, not per-SHA artifact. So running per-SHA might run outdated checks if the ref moved. Wait, B per-ref + true means only the newest run lives (cancels old ones on the same ref). But \"Kimi praktisch: ein Cancel mid-write kann inkonsistenten Zustand hinterlassen; das Gate muss `cancelled` ignorieren.\" This means cancelling (true) is dangerous because it can leave inconsistent state. Therefore, A or B with `true` is risky. But wait, what is the verdict? The evidence says \"in einer Concurrency-Gruppe löscht ein neuer Lauf die wartenden Vorgänger, `cancel-in-progress: false` schützt nur den laufenden.\" If we use `true`, we cancel waiting AND running? The text says `cancel-in-progress: false` schützt nur den laufenden (implies `true` cancels the running one too). Kimi says cancel mid-write leaves inconsistent state. So `true` is problematic. But the options are A per-SHA + true, B per-ref + true, C Deb","reasoning_content":"The user wants me to act as a council session in the house of \"omegaflow\" (A = A; 0 honored: null-echt | absent | riss; keine harten Schwellen; PII bleibt im Haus).\nThe task is to address three open architecture questions for the control center.\nFor each question:\n(1) Verdict A/B/C(/D)\n(2) Two to three sentences of justification\n(3) The strongest rift/counter-evidence\n(4) If uncertain, explicitly state \"pending\" + what is missing to measure.\n\nImportant: Do not invent facts. Numbers and quotes only from the given evidence. Answer in German. Max ~700 words.\n\nLet's analyze the input evidence for each question.\n\nQuestion 1 — R-2026-10-18-a: Where is the receipt documented in the Token-Ledger?\nCurrent state: Token state (xfer) only lives in the sender's ledger (Emitter). Recipient cannot acknowledge their own input: `xfer close` on a foreign token is a hard error. Receipt and self-completion are both `CLOSED` - indistinguishable. Also `register_lookup --addressed <linie>` lists closed blocks further; `--fired` stays 0, completed reads as open. An early built `xfer ack` arm was reverted (Commit eb2f1b749, −202 lines).\nOptions:\nA — own act `ACK <tok> by=<linie>.<sitz>` in recipient's ledger; ACKED distinguishable from CLOSED.\nB — as today: recipient writes CLOSED into Emitter ledger.\nC — no new act: receipt state is read-only derived from token ledger.\nD — a ledger per token instead of per line (both write same store).\nSecond question `--addressed` (independent): S1 hide closed blocks · S2 continue listing, but with state column.\n\nQuestion 2 — R3 from R-2026-10-17-a: Concurrency key of `register-coverage`\nShould `register-coverage.yml` switch to `group: register-coverage-${{ github.sha }}` with `cancel-in-progress: true` (like `ci-gate` since 2026-10-08) instead of today's `group: …-${{ github.ref }}` with `false`?\nMeasured: a push on main fires 5–7 gates; last 100 runs = 25 different HEAD-SHAs; in 10 minutes eight `register-coverage` runs, seven `cancelled` — in a concurrency group a new run deletes waiting predecessors, `cancel-in-progress: false` only protects the running one. The gate checks the HEAD state of the register, no per-SHA artifact.\nPreliminary: 3:3 tie. A per-SHA (GLM, DeepSeek, Lumo: \"HEAD is defined per SHA\") · B per-ref (Fugu, Mistral, Kimi: \"address of HEAD is ref, per-SHA addresses the past\"). Kimi practical: a cancel mid-write can leave inconsistent state; the gate must ignore `cancelled`.\nOptions: A per-SHA + true · B per-ref + true (only newest run lives) · C Debounce/Batch.\n\nQuestion 3 — R-2026-10-18-e Folge: The register does not know `state/stimmen`\n`register_lookup` (Rust) searches `docs/{handover,surveys,plans,auftrag,blatt,concepts,paper}` — but NOT `state/stimmen/`, where all council verdicts lie (measured 2026-10-18: `grep -c stimmen` = 0). Consequence: a line asks \"what is open for me?\" and sees foreign verdicts only if the control center manually distributes them as a letter.\nLines may not change architecture themselves (R-2026-10-18-d/e: architecture paths need a RAT-ID). The verdict format is: `## An <linie>` blocks at the end of the verdict file.\nOptions:\nA — `state/stimmen/` as additional document root in the reader.\nB — derive: verdicts migrate as `## An <linie>` blocks into the living handover of the recipient line.\nC — a new register document `state/stimmen/_register.md` (index only) as own source.\nD — status quo: control center distributes manually.\nAdditional question: Must the election itself stand as RAT-ID in the index before it is built?\n\nWait, what is the requested format?\nF1 Verdikt: <A|B|C|D|pending> [+ S1|S2] — Begründung: … — Riss: …\nF2 Verdikt: … — Begründung: … — Riss: …\nF3 Verdikt: … — Begründung: … — Riss: … — Zusatz: ja/nein, weil …\n\nLet's construct the answers based strictly on the text.\n\nFrage 1:\nThe problem states: Recipient