 (`CLOSED`) unterscheidbar wird, was den harten Fehler bei fremden Tokens behebt. S2 listet geschlossene Blöcke weiter, zeigt aber den Zustand an, sodass Erledigtes nicht mehr fälschlich als offen (`--fired` = 0) gelesen wird.\nRiss: Ein früher gebauter `xfer ack`-Arm wurde im Commit eb2f1b749 (−202 Zeilen) zurückgenommen, was den Aufwand für A und die Unsicherheit über dessen Stabilität erhöht.\n\nQuestion 2:\nMeasured: Push on main fires 5–7 gates; last 100 runs = 25 different HEAD-SHAs; in 10 mins 8 runs, 7 cancelled — in a concurrency group a new run deletes waiting predecessors, `cancel-in-progress: false` only protects the running one. The gate checks HEAD state of register, no per-SHA artifact.\nOptions: A per-SHA + true · B per-ref + true (only newest run lives) · C Debounce/Batch.\nWait, if the gate checks the HEAD state (no per-SHA artifact), then a per-SHA concurrency group (A) with `cancel-in-progress: true` would mean different SHAs run concurrently or cancel each other? Wait. If group is per-SHA, and we push 5-7 gates, and there are 25 HEAD-SHAs in 100 runs, then per-SHA groups mean each SHA gets its own group. But the gate checks HEAD state, not per-SHA artifact. So running per-SHA might run outdated checks if the ref moved. Wait, B per-ref + true means only the newest run lives (cancels old ones on the same ref). But \"Kimi praktisch: ein Cancel mid-write kann inkonsistenten Zustand hinterlassen; das Gate muss `cancelled` ignorieren.\" This means cancelling (true) is dangerous because it can leave inconsistent state. Therefore, A or B with `true` is risky. But wait, what is the verdict? The evidence says \"in einer Concurrency-Gruppe löscht ein neuer Lauf die wartenden Vorgänger, `cancel-in-progress: false` schützt nur den laufenden.\" If we use `true`, we cancel waiting AND running? The text says `cancel-in-progress: false` schützt nur den laufenden (implies `true` cancels the running one too). Kimi says cancel mid-write leaves inconsistent state. S